<p>Freeze damage insurance claims in Dallas tend to arrive all at once. A few nights in the teens or single digits can split pipes across the county, and the water damage often shows up only after temperatures rise and the ice in the line thaws. Many North Texas homes have plumbing in attics, exterior walls, slab penetrations, and crawl spaces that were never designed for a long hard freeze.</p>
<p>This page explains how freeze and burst-pipe claims usually work, the policy conditions insurers focus on, and how a public adjuster in Dallas can help you document the damage and review the settlement.</p>

<h2>Winter Storms in Dallas County</h2>
<p><strong>Winter Storm Uri</strong>, February 13&ndash;19, 2021, is the event most Dallas homeowners remember. NOAA records describe 2 to 5 inches of snow on February 14&ndash;15, followed by another round of snow, sleet, and ice (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=944869" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event</a>). Lows fell into the single digits on February 15&ndash;16, and wind chills ranged from 0 to &minus;15&deg;F (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=942630" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event</a>). Burst pipes were widespread across North Texas.</p>
<p>Uri was not the only one. NOAA also lists an ice storm on February 1, 2023, winter storms in February 2022, a winter storm on January 9, 2025, and a winter storm with extreme cold on January 24, 2026 (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1313571" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event</a>). Ice storms bring their own damage: heavy limbs breaking onto roofs, cars, fences, and power lines. Our <a href="/storm-history/">storm history</a> page lists more winter events.</p>

<h2>Common Types of Freeze Damage</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Burst supply lines</strong> in attics, exterior walls, and under sinks on outside walls.</li>
<li><strong>Fire sprinkler lines</strong> in homes, apartments, and commercial buildings, which can release a large volume of water.</li>
<li><strong>Water heaters and fixtures</strong> in garages or attics, plus frozen outdoor hose bibs that crack inside the wall.</li>
<li><strong>Pier-and-beam crawl spaces</strong>, common in older neighborhoods such as Lakewood, the M Streets, and Oak Cliff, where exposed pipes run under the floor.</li>
<li><strong>Ice-loaded trees and limbs</strong> falling onto roofs and structures.</li>
<li><strong>Roof leaks</strong> from ice buildup and damaged roofing after a storm.</li>
<li><strong>Pool equipment and irrigation backflow devices</strong>, which may have separate limits or exclusions.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What Insurers Look For on Freeze Claims</h2>
<p>Freeze claims often turn on a few policy conditions. Insurers commonly ask:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Was heat maintained?</strong> Many policies exclude freeze damage unless you used reasonable care to keep the building heated or shut off and drained the water.</li>
<li><strong>Was the home vacant?</strong> Vacancy clauses can restrict coverage for homes left unoccupied for a period stated in the policy.</li>
<li><strong>How long did water leak?</strong> Many policies exclude continuous or repeated seepage over time, so slow leaks discovered late can draw a denial.</li>
<li><strong>What failed?</strong> The cost to fix the pipe itself may be treated differently from the resulting water damage.</li>
</ul>
<p>Power outages complicate the &ldquo;heat maintained&rdquo; question, as many Dallas homeowners learned in 2021. If heat was lost because of an outage, document the outage dates and times. Outage notices from Oncor, text alerts, and dated photos of thermostats can help.</p>

