<h2>Documenting a Freeze or Burst Pipe Claim</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Evidence</th><th>Why it helps</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Photos of the broken pipe and water damage</td><td>Shows where the failure occurred and how far water spread.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Plumber&rsquo;s invoice and report</td><td>Confirms the pipe froze and burst and when it was repaired.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mitigation and drying logs</td><td>Moisture readings show affected materials and drying progress.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Thermostat, utility, or outage records</td><td>Helps show heat was maintained or explains why it was lost.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Contents list</td><td>Damaged furniture, flooring, clothing, and electronics.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Weather records</td><td>NOAA data confirming freezing temperatures on the date of loss.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> walks through what to save.</p>

<h2>Water Damage After a Freeze</h2>
<p>A burst pipe is really a water damage claim. Water can soak drywall, insulation, hardwood, laminate, cabinets, and subfloors, and it can travel along slab floors or into crawl spaces before anyone notices. Fast extraction and drying help limit damage and mold. Make sure the estimate includes everything the water reached, not just the room where the pipe failed. See our <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a> page for more on scoping water losses.</p>
<p>In apartments, condos, and multi-unit buildings, a burst line on an upper floor can damage several units below. The building owner&rsquo;s policy generally covers the structure, while residents usually need their own renters or condo policy for belongings and improvements. Each party should document its own damage and report it to its own insurer.</p>

<h2>Disputes and Underpayments</h2>
<p>When a hard freeze produces a wave of claims at once, inspections can be rushed. Common problems include drying allowances that are too short, flooring replaced in only part of a continuous area, cabinets and countertops left out, and contents underpaid. If your payment does not cover the repairs, an <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid claim review</a> or a <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">supplement</a> may help. A denial based on the heat-maintenance or seepage clauses deserves a careful look at the actual policy language; see <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a>.</p>

<h2>How Dallas Public Adjuster Can Help</h2>
<p>Dallas Public Adjuster, a service of Rise Public Adjusting LLC, represents policyholders on freeze and water losses across Dallas, Dallas County, and surrounding North Texas communities. We can review your policy conditions, document the damage, prepare an Xactimate estimate, and communicate with your insurer. We cannot guarantee a result. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<h2>When a Pipe Bursts</h2>
<ol>
<li>Shut off the main water valve. Dallas Water Utilities serves the City of Dallas; know where your shutoff is before winter.</li>
<li>Turn off electricity to wet areas if it is safe.</li>
<li>Photograph the damage and the broken pipe, then call a plumber and a mitigation company.</li>
<li>Report the claim promptly and keep every receipt.</li>
<li>Keep the damaged pipe section if the plumber removes it.</li>
</ol>
<p>For legal questions about a denial or deadlines, consult a Texas attorney.</p>
